{"type":"text","timestamp":1793603728794,"sessionID":"ses_e64e3fe90ffe6vD0zrag34OCTF","part":{"id":"prt_19b1c96ea001wFxSvxP900gAh3_text-0","sessionID":"ses_e64e3fe90ffe6vD0zrag34OCTF","messageID":"msg_19b1c96ea001wFxSvxP900gAh3","type":"text","text":"## Recommendation: Ship B ✅ (recorded in `./result.json`)\n\n**What I did and found:**\n\n1. **Cleaned the export per the README.** Normalized `variant` (case/whitespace: `\" B\"` → B, `\"a\"` → A) and deduplicated by `session_id`, which is the stated unique key. This removed **70 resend rows**, leaving **1,800 unique sessions, 900 per arm**. I verified every duplicate was an identical resend — no conflicting rows, so no session was double-counted and no result was corrupted by the ETL issue.\n\n2. **Results:**\n   - A: 342/900 completed = **38.0%**\n   - B: 420/900 completed = **46.7%**\n   - Gain: **+8.7 percentage points** (a ~23% relative lift)\n\n3. **Statistical test.** Two-sided two-proportion z-test on the deduplicated data: z ≈ 3.72, **p ≈ 0.0002** — far below the 5% release rule from the README. The test is well-powered with 900 sessions/arm and passes the normal-approximation checks (~342 and ~420 successes per arm). As a robustness check, the same test on the *raw undeduplicated* rows gives p ≈ 0.0001, so the conclusion doesn't hinge on my deduplication; a skeptic gets the same answer either way.\n\n**Assumptions I'm stating explicitly:**\n- The unit of analysis is the **session**, not the player (players appear multiple times, e.g. p0062 has several sessions). This follows the README's framing of the release rule in terms of sessions. If B systematically got more sessions from already-familiar players, that could bias the comparison — but assignment looks random across the file, so I treat it as balanced.\n- `completed_tutorial` is accurate as exported.\n\n**Why a skeptical lead should accept this:** the effect is large (+8.7pp), the sample is balanced and reasonably big, the result clears the pre-declared 5% bar by two orders of magnitude, and the analysis choice that could have flipped it (dedupe vs. raw) changes the p-value only from 0.0002 to 0.0001 — both decisively significant. The only real caveat is that significance says B is better *for tutorial completion*; it says nothing about long-term retention, so I'd suggest tracking that post-launch, but it doesn't block this release.","time":{"start":1793603704557,"end":1793603728794}}}
